    12	# Design analysis for the D5 variant
    13	# - Source-task review: no separate source_task was provided, so only the original
    14	#   dbff022c solver/generator and its official examples were used as the baseline.
    15	# - Concrete behavior:
    16	#   The palette mechanism stays unchanged: a 2xN or Nx2 legend on one outer edge
    17	#   maps sprite border colors to interior fill colors. The changed part is what the
    18	#   output hole looks like. Instead of turning every interior background cell into a
    19	#   single solid color, each enclosed hole becomes alternating stripes made from
    20	#   the mapped fill color and the background color.
    21	# - Stripe rule used here:
    22	#   For one enclosed hole, compute its local bounding box. If the hole is wider
    23	#   than tall, or square, use vertical stripes. If the hole is taller than wide,
    24	#   use horizontal stripes. The first stripe inside the local bbox is always the
    25	#   mapped fill color, and the next stripe is background, alternating by one cell.
    26	#   This makes the branch choice visible from geometry rather than from invisible
    27	#   metadata such as color ids or generation order.
    28	# - Behavior trace on one hole:
    29	#   Original task: a 2x4 hole adjacent to border color 6 and palette value 3 would
    30	#   become all 3s.
    31	#   Variant: the same 2x4 hole is wider than tall, so it becomes vertical stripes:
    32	#   columns 0 and 2 use color 3, columns 1 and 3 stay background 0.
    33	#   The solver still detects the hole and the dominant surrounding border color in
    34	#   exactly the same way; only the writeback step changes.
    35	# - Complexity calibration:
    36	#   The variant keeps only two stripe orientations, derived from visible geometry.
    37	#   That is enough variety to avoid degeneration into the original task, while still
    38	#   being learnable from a few examples. The generator deliberately produces filled
    39	#   sprites whose holes support both horizontal and vertical cases across samples.
    40	# - Implicit assumptions from the original main() that needed review:
    41	#   1. The palette-detection code assumes the palette itself is unchanged and still
    42	#      isolated on a grid edge. This remains true.
    43	#   2. The hole-filling routine originally assumed every cell in a hole could be
    44	#      overwritten with the same fill color. That assumption no longer holds, so the
    45	#      per-cell writeback must depend on hole-local row/column parity.
    46	#   3. The original code safely computed all bg components before mutating the grid.
    47	#      This is still required, because striped filling intentionally leaves some bg
    48	#      cells behind; recomputing components after each fill would split regions and
    49	#      corrupt processing.
    50	#   4. The adjacent-border-color vote must still be taken from the pre-filled hole
    51	#      boundary, not from newly written stripe cells. This code keeps that ordering.
    52	# - Placement legality and solver/generator agreement:
    53	#   The generator keeps the original non-overlap and 1-cell separation constraints
